A||||4||Blacktree||Advancing / retarding valve timing?||||1||
Z||000000||Blacktree||12-19-2001||01:58 AM||||I just got a Cloyes double-roller timing set for my 2.8 rebuild/tweak project. I noticed that in addition to the standard timing marks there also two other marks on the cam gear, allowing the valve timing to be advanced or retarded by 6 degrees. So that got me wondering... exactly what happens when you advance or retard the valve timing?<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.tweaktech.com" TARGET=_blank>Tweaktech</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Blacktree||12-20-2001||12:34 AM||||(bump)<P>anyone?||||reg||1
Z||000002||SKIDMARK||12-20-2001||01:01 AM||||I can't swear by it but I have heard from others that changing the valve timing on these engines (2.8, 3.1, 3.4) really doesn't give any benifit. There was a perfomance review I read a while ago that outlined modifications/perfomance gains and they only noted a minimal gain of less than 2hp by changing the valve timing. They also stated that GM engineers did a pretty good job on the valve timing set up. The review was done by a race team running a stock cam. Whatever class they were running wouldn't allow a modified cam. Hope this helps||||reg||1
Z||000003||Blacktree||12-20-2001||01:03 AM||||Thanks! That's more than I knew 10 minutes ago.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||G-Nasty||12-20-2001||01:08 AM||||The retarded engine also runs hotter with the gain.||||reg||1
